<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Quantitative comparison with the state-of-the-arts. LAIONs-based Score and MLLMs-based Score are scored by LAIONS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2024</a>)</cite> and MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al. 2024a</a>)</cite>, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data.</span>
Considering the significant differences in aesthetic analysis between portraits and other categories of images, it is not suitable for processing as a general aesthetic enhancement task. Specifically, we have deleted the training samples containing highly personalized semantic content such as “portrait” and “people”. Ultimately, We collected 47.5K samples from AVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Murray et al. 2012</a>)</cite>, TAD66K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, KONIQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hosu et al. 2020</a>)</cite> and FLICRK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, consisting of 45K training and 1.5K testing samples. Each sample contains a input image and a reference image, a caption (brief overview of image content) and an textual aesthetic assessment of the input image.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span>
We evaluate the performance of the generation models in terms of image aesthetic quality and image content consistency.
For image aesthetic quality assessment, we employ the mainstream aesthetic scoring models, LAION-Aesthetic Predictors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schuhmann 2024</a>)</cite> to score the results (LAIONs-based Score: the average score of testing data, which is scaled to (0,10)). We also adopt MLLMs, UNIAA-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and DepictQA-v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al. 2024a</a>)</cite> for scoring the results (MLLMs-based Score: the average score of testing data, which is scaled to (0,5)).
For image content consistency, we utilize the CLIP-I score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hessel et al. 2022</a>)</cite> to evaluate the similarity between the result image and the input image.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11556v1/fig4_viz_sub1_c.png" id="Sx4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/379;" width="698" height="379" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Examples of generative results from low aesthetics quality batch (MOS <math id="Sx4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;4.0</annotation></semantics></math>). The examples with resolution of <math id="Sx4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math>, while the Mean Aesthetic Score (MAS) below is the LAIONs-based Score calculated on the batch.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Comparison with State-of-the-Arts</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect different SOTA diffusion-based image editing methods using Stable Diffusion v1.5 as diffusion backbone, ControlNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, InstructPix2Pix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, MGIE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, DOODL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al. 2023b</a>)</cite> as baselines. We offer different suitable prompts for the SOTA methods to achieve image aesthetic enhancements (shown in Appendix).
We also discussed in more detail the performance of DIAE under different original image qualities. We divided the testing images into two batches: one batch represents low aesthetic quality samples with LAIONs-based Mean Aesthetic Score (MAS) below 4.0, and the other batch represents high aesthetic quality samples with MAS above 5.0. We selected some samples from the two batches, as shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Comparison with State-of-the-Arts ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and calculated the MAS of the results generated by different methods on these two batches.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11556v1/fig4_viz_sub2_c.png" id="Sx4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/358;" width="698" height="358" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Examples of generative results from high aesthetics quality batch (MOS <math id="Sx4.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;5.0</annotation></semantics></math>). The examples with resolution of <math id="Sx4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math>, while the Mean Aesthetic Score (MAS) below is the LAIONs-based Score calculated on the batch.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Aesthetics Comparison.</span>
As Tab. <a href="#Sx4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shown, DIAE achieve the highest aesthetic scores in both the LAIONs-based Scores and Aesthetics-MLLMs Scores. For <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> images, DIAE’s LAIONs-based Score and MLLMs-based Score improved by 7.3% and 3.0%, respectively.
For <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> images, DIAE’s LAIONs-based Scores and MLLMs-based Scores improved by 17.4% and 11.0%, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, for images with very poor aesthetic quality (MAS<math id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;4.0</annotation></semantics></math>), these images have obvious color and brightness defects. Additionally, images with no color intensity variation lead to a lack of the targets of images. The images in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> obtain the image aesthetic assessments from MLLM (UNIAA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. 2024</a>)</cite>), such as <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Undersaturation”</span>, <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Poor light”</span>, and <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“No target in the image.”</span>. We performed inference on these images using DIAE and other SOTA methods. It can be seen that compared to other methods, DIAE shows significant advantages in image color and brightness, and the noticeable variation in color intensity further achieves image re-targeting.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Comparison with State-of-the-Arts ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, for images with slightly better aesthetic quality (MAS<math id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;5.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>5.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;5.0</annotation></semantics></math>), these images do not have obvious defects in color, brightness, composition, etc. Such images require further optimization of color contrast and brightness contrast to make the focus of the image more prominent and beautified. We also performed inference on these images using DIAE and other SOTA methods. It can be seen that compared to other methods, DIAE achieves a higher increase in MOS scores, with better preservation of original image details and higher color authenticity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Content Consistency Comparison.</span> The image content consistency is scored by CLIP-I Score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hessel et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, which can measure the semantic similarity between the input image and the generated result.
As Fig. <a href="#Sx4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shown, the CLIP scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hessel et al. 2022</a>)</cite> of DIAE achieved 0.772 and 0.784 on <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512\times 512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>512</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512\times 512</annotation></semantics></math> images, respectively, which outperform the other methods.
As Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Comparison with State-of-the-Arts ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> demonstrated, our DIAE shows advantages in preserving the content details of the original image compared to other methods. Unlike other methods (such as the first-row <span id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cat</span> image in Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> by other methods), our DIAE dose not add or remove details from the original image. Our method also does not create something out of nothing (such as InstructPix2Pix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brooks et al. 2023</a>)</cite> generating completely unrelated buildings in the second row of Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Experiments Settings ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Study on the value of <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
The parameter <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> determines how much the DIAE’s generated image guided by the input image or the reference image. Specifically, a smaller <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> increases supervision by the reference image during more denoising timesteps, while a larger <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces this supervision, allowing greater focus on the input image. We have conducted extensive experiments to evaluate the impact of <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> on generated results. As Fig. <a href="#Sx4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Ablation Study ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> demonstrated, as <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the generated images retain more aesthetic elements, such as color and brightness, from the input image(Note that the total number of timesteps is 1000). We also computed the mean CLIP-I Scores of different generated results based on different <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results indicate the larger <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates rentaining more details of input images, and adjusting <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> influences the aesthetic attributes of generated images.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.11556v1/fig5_ablation2.png" id="Sx4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:698/649;" width="698" height="649" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Comparison between DIAE with different <math id="Sx4.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. CLIP-I: the similarity of generated and input images.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Multimodal aesthetic perception.</span>
We evaluate the impact of MAP on DIAE by withdrawing the visual modality guidance and textual modality guidance from MAP separately. When the visual modality is withdrawed from MAP and a text prompt completely consistent with MAP is provided as guidance (requiring a contour map, as it is a necessary input for ControlNet), DIAE degrades to ControlNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>)</cite> in the comparative experiment, called DIAE (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-w/o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-w/o</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>). As shown in tab. <a href="#Sx4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Ablation Study ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, its performance in aesthetic enhancement and original content preservation is inferior to DIAE. When the text modality is withdrawed from MAP and HSV and contour maps completely consistent with MAP are provided as guidance (the text prompt only includes the image caption, i.e., content semantics, without any aesthetic text evaluation), called DIAE (<math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-w/o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-w/o</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>). As shown in tab. <a href="#Sx4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Ablation Study ‣ Experiments ‣ Enhancing Image Aesthetics with Dual-Conditioned Diffusion Models Guided by Multimodal Perception" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, its aesthetic enhancement is also inferior to DIAE. However, it still has advantages over other methods, especially in content consistency, indicating that introducing dual visual modalities as guidance further enhances the model’s advantage in content consistency of the generated results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T2" class="ltx_table">
